      "user_input": "request.get?",
      "confidence": "Weak",
      "note": "False positive. This check flags a GET-only guard that a HEAD request slips past. Here the guard protects nothing security-relevant: store_location only remembers a path in the session so redirect_back_or can send the user where they were headed after signing in. A HEAD request simply does not get a return location stored - no authorization is bypassed and no state is changed. Nothing downstream trusts session[:return_to] beyond redirect_to."
